# Single-qubit deterministic benchmarking (1qb-DB)

1qb-DB repeats identical \(X_\pi\)-pulse pairs to amplify coherent pulse error.
It compares a Gaussian pulse with a robust, strictly resonant composite pulse.

## Protocol

The control is

$$
H_c(t)=\frac{\Omega_x(t)X+\Omega_y(t)Y}{2},
\qquad
(\Omega_x,\Omega_y)\rightarrow(1+\epsilon)(\Omega_x,\Omega_y),
$$

with \(\Delta(t)=0\). Prepare \(|0\rangle\), apply \(n\) identical \(XX\)
cycles, and measure

$$
P_0(n,\epsilon)
=|\langle0|(X_\epsilon X_\epsilon)^n|0\rangle|^2.
$$

Use \(\epsilon=\pm3\%\) and \(n=0,\ldots,60\). For an ideal pulse \(P_0=1\).
For the Gaussian area-error model,

$$
P_0(n,\epsilon)=\cos^2(n\pi\epsilon),
$$

so coherent error appears as an oscillation. A robust pulse should suppress
this variation.

## Files

- [`1qb-DB-test.nb`](1qb-DB-test.nb): analytic Mathematica model.
- [`X_gate_pulse_generation/`](X_gate_pulse_generation/): saved Gaussian and
  resonant robust waveforms.
- [`1qb-DB-demo/`](1qb-DB-demo/): completed waveform-level DB simulation.

## Status and next steps

1. **Waveforms — complete.** Gaussian and reoptimized resonant composite
   candidates are saved and reproducible.
2. **Simulation — complete.** The notebook injects error into the sampled
   Hamiltonian, calculates the noisy gate, and plots \(P_0(n)\) through
   60 cycles.
3. **Hardware conversion — next.** Choose \(T_g\), sample rate, and amplitude limit;
   then convert normalized controls to device units.
4. **Experiment.** Run both pulses with the same calibration and fit the
   residual coherent error and \(\Delta P_0\).
